## 3. Empirical validation

The formula was tested against `NB_MATURITE_IRBA` on the type 1 population in `srs_X_capret` (n = 7,042 facilities).

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| RMSE | 29.2 days |
| MAE | 8.6 days |
| Mean bias | −7.7 days |
| Median bias | −3.2 days |
| % match \|err\| < 0.05 yrs | 95.21% |
| % match \|err\| < 0.1 yrs | **96.55%** |
| % \|err\| > 0.1 yrs | 3.45% |

The formula reproduces 95% of the population within 18 days. The small **negative** bias of ~8 days reflects calendar-day rounding: SRS uses actual payment dates, while the formula assumes evenly spaced months from the reporting date. The bias direction is opposite to the type 3 case because the linear amortization profile front-loads more weight on early payments than the geometric profile of constant annuities.

## 4. Stability check across maturity bands

| Band R | n | RMSE (days) | Mean bias (days) |
|---|---|---|---|
| [0, 1[ | 997 | 35.98 | −10.92 |
| [1, 3[ | 1,709 | 41.66 | −12.81 |
| [3, 5[ | 1,247 | 27.46 | −7.24 |
| [5, 10[ | 1,199 | 21.71 | −5.32 |
| [10, 15[ | 953 | 15.20 | −4.08 |
| [15, 20[ | 581 | 3.35 | −2.48 |
| [20, 30[ | 356 | 3.14 | −2.55 |

RMSE decreases monotonically with maturity above the [1, 3[ band: 42 days on the [1, 3[ band, down to **~3 days on long maturities (≥15 yrs)**. The convergence to ~3 days on long maturities confirms the formula is structurally exact — the residual error on shorter bands is a discretization and calendar effect that diminishes when $n$ is large.

The slight RMSE increase between [0, 1[ and [1, 3[ (36 → 42 days) reflects the contribution of the residual outlier cluster (see section 6), which is concentrated on short-to-medium maturities.

## 5. No bascule rule for type 1

Unlike type 3, where short facilities (R ≤ 1) systematically fall back to NB = R, the type 1 population does not exhibit this behavior. On the 997 facilities with R ≤ 1, only **19.0% bascule** (i.e. NB ≈ R); the remainder follows the linear formula. The formula is therefore applied **without override** for type 1.

## 6. Residual outlier cluster

The error distribution on type 1 reveals a small cluster (~3.2%, 226 facilities) with errors centered on **−180 to −60 days** — the same hidden-typology offset observed on the progressive (type 3) population. Distribution of errors in days:

| Bin (days) | n | % |
|---|---|---|
| [−180, −60[ | 226 | **3.21%** |
| [−60, −30[ | 43 | 0.61% |
| [−30, −10[ | 9 | 0.13% |
| [−10, 0[ | 6,368 | **90.43%** |
| [0, +10[ | 331 | 4.70% |
| [+10, +30[ | 65 | 0.92% |

90.4% of errors are within ±10 days — pure calendar noise. The 3.2% cluster on the negative tail corresponds to facilities where SRS reports a maturity **shorter** than the linear formula predicts. Cross-referencing with the type 3 analysis, these facilities share the same characteristics:

- Concentrated in `Type_Credit = "Crédits d'investissement"`
- Older facilities with longer T0
- Repayment profile inconsistent with a flat linear amortization

These facilities likely correspond to investment loans whose contractual schedule has been mis-flagged as `CD_TYPE_AMORT = 1`, but whose effective principal repayment differs from a true linear profile (e.g. front-loaded amortization, scheduled balloon adjustments). Identifying this sub-population would require a product-typology variable that is not available in `srs_X_capret`. As for type 3, this gap is documented as a known limitation.

